    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Eukaryotic cells have large genomes that are tightly wrapped around protein cores called histones to fit in the small nuclear compartment. However, cells also need chromatin remodeling complexes to unwind the genetic material. The yeast INO80 chromatin remodeling complex regulates the expression of genes that are involved in metabolic pathways, such as glycolysis and oxidative phosphorylation. Broadly, this research considers the relationship between the metabolism of the cell and the activity of chromatin modifying enzymes. Specifically, this research was designed to elucidate the regulation of the INO80 chromatin remodeling complex in response to nutrient stress in the yeast Saccharomyces cerevisiae. In particular, we focused on the TOR signaling pathway, which is a nutrient-responsive regulator of cell growth. Previous studies found that treatment with rapamycin or depletion of amino acids, both of which reduce TORC1 activity in the pathway, caused degradation of Ino80, the catalytic core of the INO80 complex. However, it is unknown if this also disrupts the stability of other subunits of the INO80 complex. Using Western blot analysis of epitope-tagged proteins, we analyzed the stability of the INO80 complex subunits in response to TORC1 inhibition. We find that rapamycin treatment results in specific degradation of Ino80 but no other subunits. In addition, we replicated an assay to track TORC1 activity. This study contributes to our understanding of the response of chromatin remodelers under nutrient stress, a cellular condition that can be found in disease like diabetes and cancer.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
